{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3060","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-runtime-hints-r7.md@sha256:a81cf250fde5dcff1b6dc84215941fd5a130b485851f5cca36901865813f01a5"},"owner_reference":"3060","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nR7-compliant runtime hints in `backlog_view` compact and over-budget responses\n\n## Problem Statement\n\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` builds two runtime hint strings that an\nagent reads at the moment it decides what to call next: `_build_compact_manifest()`'s `_hint`\nfield (around line 2306) and `_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` field (around line 2379).\nBoth violate R7 (\"Hints must be actionable\") in `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`:\n\n- `_hint` lists `Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)` before\n  `Load specific sections: ...` \u2014 R7 forbids a hint recommending retrieving everything as its\n  first suggestion.\n- `_usage` unconditionally says \"Use the sections_index below... then request them\n  individually\" even when the caller already narrowed to one oversized section via\n  `sections=[...]` and the over-budget response recurred for that single section \u2014 advice the\n  caller has already exhausted, which R7 also forbids.\n- Neither hint names the paginated navigation mechanism (`navigate=`, `head=`, `skip_tokens=`)\n  that `disclosure_handler.py`'s `next_call` hint (around line 487-489) already uses elsewhere\n  in the same package for the identical page-through problem \u2014 so the mechanism that actually\n  solves \"content too big for one response\" is invisible at the two call sites where a caller\n  first learns their response was withheld or truncated.\n\nAgents follow these strings operationally; documentation is a weaker lever because the hint is\nread on every call, at the exact moment of the routing decision. A hint that leads with \"load\neverything\" steers agents toward defeating the token-budget mechanism the compact/over-budget\nresponses exist to enforce.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. `_build_compact_manifest()`'s `_hint` does not list a full-content load as its first\n   suggested action.\n2. `_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` offers a next action the caller has not already\n   exhausted when the caller narrowed to a single section (via `sections=[...]` or\n   `section=...` naming one section) and still received an over-budget response for it.\n3. Both hints name the paginated navigation parameters (`navigate=`, `head=`,\n   `skip_tokens=`) as an available mechanism, consistent with how\n   `disclosure_handler.py`'s existing `next_call` hint already presents them.\n4. Every address referenced in a hint (a `selector=`, `section=`, or ordinal value) is valid\n   against the response it accompanies \u2014 no hint names a section or ordinal absent from that\n   response's own `sections_index`.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Not touching pagination-cache or control-set code (`disclosure_handler.py`'s\n   control-set/pagination-cache internals) \u2014 that is issue #3062's separate scope.\n2. Not changing the compact/over-budget trigger thresholds (`_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`) or when a\n   compact/over-budget response is returned at all \u2014 only the hint text within an unchanged\n   response shape.\n3. Not adding a new navigation mechanism \u2014 `navigate=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=` already exist on\n   `backlog_view`; this only makes the existing hints mention them.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. `_build_compact_manifest()` orders `_hint` so that a section-scoped or navigate-based load\n   is suggested before (or in place of, when narrower is sufficient) an unqualified\n   `summary=False` full-content load.\n2. `_build_over_budget_view()` detects when the incoming request already scoped to a single\n   section (the over-budget response recurred despite narrowing) and, in that case, offers the\n   caller `navigate=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=` paginated retrieval of that section instead of\n   repeating \"use the sections_index... request them individually.\"\n3. Both hint strings mention `navigate=`, `head=`, and `skip_tokens=` as an available retrieval\n   path, matching the parameter names and usage pattern already established in\n   `disclosure_handler.py`'s `next_call` hint construction.\n4. Hint text is generated from data already present in the same response (`selector`,\n   `sections_index`, the section(s) the caller requested) \u2014 no hint references a section name,\n   ordinal, or parameter not derivable from that response.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. No change to `backlog_view`'s existing parameters, return schema keys, or trigger\n   conditions for compact/over-budget responses \u2014 only the string content of `_hint`/`_usage`.\n2. Behavior is demonstrated by a runnable test exercising both hint-construction functions\n   directly (unit-level, not requiring a live GitHub backend) \u2014 per this repo's close criteria,\n   a passing pre-existing test suite does not by itself demonstrate the fix; a test specific to\n   R7 compliance on both hint paths is required.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n1. Whether \"narrowed caller\" detection in `_build_over_budget_view()` should key off the\n   `sections=[...]`/`section=` parameters the caller passed (available since\n   `_execute_disclosure_or_passthrough`/the view-request path already threads the selector and\n   filter through) or off `result.section_filter_miss`/`sections_index` size \u2014 the planning\n   stage should confirm which signal is already available at the call site in `server.py`\n   without new plumbing.\n2. Whether the reordering in `_hint` should present ordinal/`navigate=` guidance ahead of\n   `Load specific sections`, or whether \"sections\" and \"navigate\" are peer alternatives with\n   \"load everything\" demoted to last \u2014 R7 only requires \"not first,\" not a specific final\n   order; planning should pick the ordering that best matches how `map=True` guidance already\n   frames the three-layer disclosure pattern documented in the `backlog_view` tool docstring.\n3. Whether `disclosure_handler.py` needs a change at all, or whether it is purely a reference\n   pattern for constructing the two hint strings in `server.py` \u2014 current reading is the latter\n   (no navigation code changes required, only hint text), to be confirmed during Impact Radius.\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `_build_compact_manifest()` (around\n  line 2275-2314) and `_build_over_budget_view()` (around line 2352-2392)\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` \u2014 `next_call` hint\n  construction (around line 457-498), the paginated-navigation reference pattern\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` \u2014 R7 (Hints must be\n  actionable) and R6/R4 for table-of-contents/addressing context R7 depends on\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md` \u2014 component ownership for\n  `server.py` vs `disclosure_handler.py`\n- Parent issue #3054\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Does `_hint` currently list full-content load first? | Yes, confirmed at `server.py` `_build_compact_manifest()` \u2014 `_hint` string's first line is `Load full content: backlog_view(...summary=False)`. | Direct file read, this session |\n| Does `_usage` distinguish a caller who already narrowed to one section? | No \u2014 the string is built unconditionally regardless of the incoming request's `section`/`sections` parameters. | Direct file read, this session |\n| Does any existing code in this package already build an R7-style paginated-navigation hint? | Yes \u2014 `disclosure_handler.py`'s `next_call` hint (`backlog_view(selector=..., navigate=..., head=..., skip_tokens=...)`), used for EXTRACT-mode truncation continuation. | Direct file read, this session |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- None \u2014 the two functions, their exact violation, and the existing in-package pattern to\n  follow are all confirmed against live source; the ordering/signal-detection choices above are\n  design questions for planning, not blockers.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] Discovery captures intent \u2014 self-initialized from backlog item #3060 in `auto` mode per\n  session's Auto Mode Active directive; no interactive confirmation gate applies.\n"}